+# Full independent review: completed modular Green-ring symmetry
+
+PASS for the scoped results. The original universal finite-group symmetry question remains unsolved after five substantive author turns. No actual-group nonsymmetry counterexample is claimed, and this is not a historical novelty certification.
+
+## Exact packet and target
+
+Author manifest SHA-256: 7d766fd29851eade576d9bd125b2f6e98c0b525b8f0631e1e23ce428f13e296a. Final WIP: 59ecabf2953d6c0d39e51a341a554152927b7b6c. Reviewed the entire source scope, final result, five proofs, manifests and exact replay. No mandatory correction is required.
+
+The original OWR printed page 849 was visually inspected, including the complex-conjugation bars. The completion is the dimension-weighted complexification of the split Green ring on indecomposable characteristic-p modules. It is not the short-exact-sequence Grothendieck ring, the stable quotient, an ordinary-character ring or the further C*-completion. The module field and analytical scalar field have different roles.
+
+## Turn 1: field extension and local species
+
+For finite algebraic extension, restriction composed with extension is multiplication by the field degree on the split ring. The upper norm inequalities in both directions force equality, even if decomposition introduces repeated summands or cancellation. For arbitrary algebraic extension, finitely many decomposition matrices and isomorphisms descend to one finite subextension. Indecomposability at the larger field prevents decomposition at that subextension, and all repeated types are identified there by the chosen data. This establishes the finite-support norm equality before extending by density.
+
+Equality of norms of all powers transfers spectral radii and the symmetric radius identity. It avoids a false blanket assertion that arbitrary Banach subalgebras are inverse-closed. The countable tensor/dual closure is stable under taking product summands. Restrictions of ambient characters give the required converse, and failure of duality on one basis element gives one of the two explicit self-adjoint witnesses. The compactness criterion has the correct quantifiers over every rational positive defect and every finite subsystem. No finite truncation is promoted to an actual species.
+
+The nonstandard-involution l1(Z) control is nonsymmetric but explicitly outside the representation-ring axioms; its radius calculation is valid.
+
+## Turn 2: endotrivial and projective factors
+
+An invertible stable object is indecomposable because the stable unit k has endomorphism ring k. Krull–Schmidt and removal of projective summands therefore give unique actual cores. The stable coordinate norm yields the asserted weighted l1 algebra without an extra quotient identification. The credited gamma theorem supplies subexponential growth for each element and its inverse, forcing every bounded group character to be unitary. No character-extension theorem from subalgebras is assumed.
+
+For a p-group, the regular projective idempotent e=P/|G| gives the full/stable product splitting with the respective factor units e and 1-e. Its inverse J has precisely the norm of the stable representative plus the absolute value of its dimension sum, hence is bounded in both directions. That coordinate dimension sum is correctly not treated as a stable-ring homomorphism. The whole stable factor is not thereby proved symmetric; only the specified endotrivial/projective closed span is.
+
+## Turn 3: a real-spectrum obstruction to restriction detection
+
+Langer Proposition 2.2, printed page 5, was visually inspected. The four resolution maps match after the stated quaternion notation change. Their left multiplication is correct for right modules, and inverse-action conversion to left modules preserves the relevant tensor and duality structures. The exact F2 rank certificates and zero composites prove exactness over every characteristic-two field, not just a numerical rank estimate. Minimality and self-injectivity exclude projective summands in the syzygies. The dimension sequence then gives exact stable order four.
+
+The Fourier idempotents are nonzero actual elements because the four syzygy cores are distinct basis vectors. They force the displayed eigenvalues in the ambient stable completion; the polynomial inverse excludes other values. The full/stable split adds scalar component zero, and the dimension correction 12e is exactly (3/2)P. Restrictions to the unique nontrivial elementary abelian subgroup are k+3kC2 for both odd syzygies and 4kC2 for P, so the signed expression cancels exactly. Its spectrum {0,-2,-4} is real. It disproves signed spectral detection, not symmetry, and does not contradict the credited positive actual-module gamma detection theorem.
+
+## Turn 4: abstract axioms versus actual modules
+
+Checked Benson Definition 1.1.1, all five axioms and the strengthened unit-coefficient condition (ii'). The proposed P-twisted finite group-ring ideal and its unitization satisfy each condition: positive integral multiplication, the cubic coefficient bound, dimension multiplicativity, and the regular positive element. The Fourier eigenvalues c and d are nonzero, so the finite-dimensional algebra is semisimple. Its self-adjoint basis nevertheless has nonreal characters. This is a genuine axioms-only obstruction, including zero radical and all the stated positive-element radius identities.
+
+The separate nonrealizability proof is essential and valid. If the trivial module were projective, semisimplicity would force a trivial direct summand in each M tensor M*, contradicting the based multiplication. Otherwise the indecomposable projective cover has dimension d, and its minimal first syzygy has dimension d-1. Self-injectivity and stable indecomposability make that syzygy an actual indecomposable with no projective summand. Its dimension is absent from the proposed complete basis. This excludes based, dimension- and duality-preserving realization over every field, without an algebraic-closure assumption.
+
+## Turn 5: actual coordinate independence and the completed decomposition
+
+For elementary abelian E, the complement H times K identifies induced modules with M tensor kK. The endomorphism algebra identity is valid for these finite tensor modules. The two nilpotent radical ideals commute as ideals, their sum is nilpotent and their quotient is a division algebra. Thus induction here preserves indecomposability over arbitrary k; no absolute indecomposability assumption is hidden.
+
+Order-p restrictions distinguish the inducing subgroup: inside H they contain the nonprojective endotrivial restriction, outside H they are free by Mackey. The contained order-p subgroups determine H. For fixed H, restriction back gives a_H copies of the original core, so Krull–Schmidt distinguishes stable classes. This proves actual module-coordinate injection before applying an abstract algebra calculation.
+
+The tensor-induction multiplicity and normalization satisfy [E:HK][E:H intersect K]=[E:H][E:K]. Projective terms vanish precisely in the stable quotient. The trivial intersection is treated as zero. The Möbius transform on nontrivial subgroups is justified by the invertible finite zeta matrix, not by an assumed independence of formal idempotents.
+
+Most importantly, the completed block maps have a leading H-coordinate equal to the input and lower-level contributions bounded by restricted core dimensions. This gives the stated lower and upper norms even when lower-level classes collide. Closed range and density of projected generators prove surjectivity. The finite product inverse has the explicit sum of idempotent-norm bound, so the decomposition is a bounded Banach *-isomorphism rather than merely algebraic. Symmetry follows for the blocks and the full projective extension with correct units.
+
+The F8 two-dimensional module is a valid proper-family witness. Its generators commute and square to one; every nonidentity group element gives a nonzero multiple of J. The centralizer algebra is local, and dimension two rules out projectivity over the order-eight group. All order-two restrictions are free, distinguishing it from every induced-endotrivial coordinate. This does not invoke a false cyclic-subgroup projectivity detection theorem for a higher-rank elementary abelian group. Coordinate closure excludes the missing basis vector from the closed span. No non-Hermitian species on this module is claimed.
+
+## Sources, reproducibility and disposition
+
+Benson arXiv:2008.13155v2 is the accessible controlling accepted manuscript. Checked Definition 1.1.1, Proposition 3.4.2, Theorem 4.4.4, Proposition 4.4.6, Lemma 4.4.7 and Theorem 4.4.8 with their stated hypotheses and credited inputs. The full published 2024 Memoirs PDF was not retrieved; the older hosted manuscript is not relabeled as a later edition. Classification and cohomological growth inputs remain credited theorems, not claimed independent proofs.
+
+All 184,604 author assertions replay byte-exactly, and 117 historical manifest entries verify. Six primary PDF hashes and the complete final author whitelist were separately checked. Independent standard-library controls passed 14,871 assertions with no author imports: abstract based-ring associativity/dimension/regular-element identities, exact subgroup-lattice Möbius and index formulas, and all F8 two-by-two centralizers and order-two restrictions. These finite controls supplement, rather than replace, the infinite-dimensional proofs and external representation-theoretic inputs.
+
+Approved original disposition: unsolved, five of five. The remaining task is control of bounded species on other actual indecomposable directions. The two failed shortcuts are carefully separated from a full counterexample, and the positive families from a universal theorem. Publish frozen author and review whitelists only, with an additive current-status wrapper and surgical target queue status/turn cells. Raw sources and private working notes remain excluded.
